Batch Apex
The problem, stated exactly

@future and Queueable each run in one transaction with one set of limits. 50,000 records won't fit — 10,000-record DML ceiling, 6MB heap, 50,000-row query limit.

Batch splits the work into chunks, and each chunk is its own transaction with fresh limits. 50,000 records at 200 per chunk = 250 separate transactions, none of them near a ceiling.

The shape — three methods
apex
public class TierRecalculationBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject> {

    public Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        return Database.getQueryLocator([
            SELECT Id, Type, AnnualRevenue, Count_Contacts__c, Customer_Tier__c
            FROM Account
        ]);
    }

    public void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Account> scope) {
        AccountTriggerHandler.assignTiers(scope);
        update scope;
    }

    public void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        // runs once, after every chunk is done
    }
}

Run it with:

apex
Database.executeBatch(new TierRecalculationBatch(), 200);

What each method is for:

Method	Runs	Job
start	once	say what to process — returns a QueryLocator
execute	once per chunk	do the work on ~200 records
finish	once	cleanup, summary email, chain the next job

Database.getQueryLocator is the piece that makes this possible. A normal SOQL query caps at 50,000 rows. A QueryLocator handles 50 million — it doesn't fetch everything, it hands Salesforce a cursor to walk through in chunks.

And look at execute: it calls AccountTriggerHandler.assignTiers(scope). Zero new business logic. That method takes a List<Account> and doesn't care where the list came from — trigger, test, or batch scope. That's the Day 10 parameter decision paying off for the fourth time, and it's exactly the "80,000 accounts overnight" scenario I asked you about on Day 10's quiz.

Two things that catch people

Database.Stateful — by default, instance variables reset between chunks. Add implements Database.Batchable<sObject>, Database.Stateful and they persist, which is how you keep a running total across all 250 executions. Without it, a counter would reset to zero every chunk.

Testing — batches only process one chunk in a test, and only if you keep the data under the chunk size. So insert 10 accounts, not 10,000, and wrap Database.executeBatch in startTest/stopTest as usual.

The build

SF-011: A batch that recalculates Customer_Tier__c on every Account in the org.

Four steps:

Create TierRecalculationBatch with the three methods
execute calls assignTiers(scope) then updates
Run it from Anonymous Apex against your org's accounts — watch it work
One test: insert 10 accounts with wrong tiers set manually, run the batch, assert they're corrected

public class TierRecalculationBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject> {
    
    public Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        return Database.getQueryLocator([
            SELECT Id, Type, AnnualRevenue, Count_Contacts__c, Customer_Tier__c
            FROM Account
        ]); 
    }
    public void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Account> scope) { 
        AccountTriggerHandler.assignTiers(scope);
        update scope;
        
    }
    public void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) { 
        
    }
}

TEST CLASS

// The trigger keeps tiers correct on insert, so this test cannot simulate
// stale historical data. It proves the batch executes, chunks, and produces
// correct tiers — not that it corrects records the trigger never touched.

@isTest
public class TierRecalculationBatchTest {
    @isTest
    static void tierTest(){
        List<Account> accs = TestDataFactory.createAccounts(10, 6000000);
        insert accs;
        
        Test.startTest();
        Database.executeBatch(new TierRecalculationBatch(), 10);
        Test.stopTest();
        
        List<Account> result= [SELECT Type, AnnualRevenue, Count_Contacts__c, Customer_Tier__c
                               FROM Account];
        Map<String, Integer> tierCounts = new Map<String, Integer>();
        for (Account a : result) {
            if (tierCounts.containsKey(a.Customer_Tier__c)) {
                tierCounts.put(a.Customer_Tier__c, tierCounts.get(a.Customer_Tier__c) + 1);
            } else {
                tierCounts.put(a.Customer_Tier__c, 1);
            }
        }
        Assert.areEqual(1, tierCounts.get('Gold'), 'One account at 6M should be Gold');
        Assert.areEqual(9, tierCounts.get('Platinum'), 'Nine accounts at 12M+ should be Platinum');
        
        
    }
    
}